artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider Titan, the largest moon of Saturn, whose atmosphere supports a methane-based hydrological cycle analogous to Earth's water cycle. Methane condenses in the troposphere to form clouds, and precipitation can either reach the surface as rain or evaporate in the atmosphere as virgae before touching the ground. Surface observations reveal dry equatorial regions, a pronounced hemispheric asymmetry in the distribution of hydrocarbon lakes and seas (with most concentrated near the north pole), and geomorphological evidence of pluvial and fluvial erosion.

Determine, quantitatively:
(a) the global frequency, intensity, and spatial distribution of methane precipitation events on Titan, distinguishing between rainfall reaching the surface and virgae that evaporates aloft;
(b) how these precipitation patterns govern rates of pluvial and fluvial erosion of Titan's surface;
(c) how precipitation controls the replenishment of Titan's lakes and seas;
(d) the mechanism by which the precipitation distribution produces the observed dry equatorial belt and the north–south hemispheric asymmetry of standing liquid bodies; and
(e) the role of these processes in closing Titan's overall methane climate cycle (sources, sinks, and atmospheric/surface reservoir balance).

